A||N||9||1FST2M6||Header numbers...  ||||1||
Z||000000||1FST2M6||06-10-2000||07:32 PM||||well guys/gal I ran my car on the Dyno today I made 4 runs and an all gear... the first pulls were within 2% of each other so i took the better of them to post... With  more duration and more over lap on the cam i could regain the lost low end tq. but the increas in high RPM HP is nice... You CAN "feel" the difference in the car... BUT, the 4:10 makes up for it in time to mph. I was unable to use the nitrous cause it's off the car. With a 3.4 these headers would be WAY better on performance than our 2.8. We just don't flow enough air to matter... and the back pressure the stock ported units make are worth the trouble... not so sure about the headers... the car sounds AWESOME and idles smoother (we can see it on a SUN scope and on a Matco Reality II)<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6headerdyno.jpg">  <P>If i Had a 3.4 I'd be please with them.. but i don't.. i'm not so sure the $720 i spent was worth my money...   OH well   what do you think??<P>they are West Coast Fieros full header set with Jet Hot Barrier coating.  <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 06-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||06-10-2000||09:34 PM||||Interesting numbers, but I think I'll stick with the ported exhaust manifolds.  It's nice to someone posting actual dyno results for these types of mods. ||||reg||14
Z||000002||batboy||06-10-2000||10:47 PM||||Sorry 1FST2M6, longer cam durations kill low end torque, but gain upper rpm horsepower.||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||06-10-2000||10:56 PM||||heh.. more lift and over lap...  OH hell.. I need a 3.4TDC!  anyone know if they are a chain drive or belt driven cams?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||06-10-2000||11:20 PM||||The TDC 3.4 is belt driven and that's it's biggest weakness. You must change them regularly because if you don't, they will eventually leave you stranded.||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||06-11-2000||09:48 AM||||COOL! so it will be  quiet... sweet.. Don't have problem changing belts.. I have to have the timing belt on my Alfa changed ever 25-30,000. Or it gets noisey and will snap and eat a $4500 pair of heads! they are impressive so see at the dealer.. the people that didn't follow Alfa Romeos warnings... ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Shaun41178||06-11-2000||03:25 PM||||i would like to say Thanks Travis for posting and testing these headers.  It gives an accurate representation of the increase that they provide or take away.<P>I do have to add my two cents to the discussion here.  Up to 4000 rpm the hp is downa few hp.  but pot really enough to make a diffrence in track times.  The reason why the Hp is down is because the torque is down as well.  more airflow via cam or forced induction will get them back up there.  From 4000 rpm on up the headers do make an improvement.  Yes it is only a 7 hp peak gain but look at the difference at 5500 rpm.  Its  at 125 hp with the headers and looks to me like 115 or so without the headers.  Difference of about 10 hp there.  I would guess too that as the rpms climbed higehr the headers would flow better.  One must also remember that Travis has like 97k miles on his engine and it is the original engine.  Compression will be down and tolerances are a bit looser so it won't make as much power as a 2.8 with 5k miles on it.<P>With a 3.2/3.4 and a high lift cam I would think you could see +15 hp gain with these headers.  The stock manifolds won't be good enough with a 3.2 or 3.4  power would really drop off in the upper rpms.  And just think if you had forced induction via nitrous or the RSM supercharger kit?  headers would be a necessity then.  You would be flowing the same amount of air or more as a Lt-1 engine at that point.  Think the stock manifolds could keep up with that?  no way.<P>But anyways, thanks Travis for posting your results to the forum.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||06-12-2000||08:34 AM||||compression test done 2 weeks ago...<P>#1- 205     #2- 190<BR>#3- 205     #4- 200<BR>#5- 185     #6- 180<P>#s in PSI  (pounds per square inch)+/-2psi done at Engineered Performance, the day I finished the tranny and header install... Not sure what the factory numbers should be I just wanted to make sure they were at least KINDA close to one another. What we think the cluperit of the lower #s on the last two are the over filling of nitrous on this cylinders cause their he first to get nailed! She still seems to pull well thought... as you can see above. ||||reg||1
